      "id": "split-error-retry",
      "text": "Start with ScanBytes and a reader that returns \"ab\" with no error. The first Scan returns true with \"a\". On the next call, let the splitter return a custom error on \"b\". Scan returns false, Err reports that error, and Text still returns \"a\". On a third call, the splitter receives the same pending byte \"b\" with atEOF=true. If it returns (1, data[:1], nil), Scan returns true with \"b\" and Err still reports the custom error.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 160,
        "fragment": "s.setErr(err)"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Should a split error make subsequent Scan calls return false, given that the SplitFunc documentation says scanning stops on error?"
    },
    {
      "id": "final-token-error-order",
      "text": "Use a splitter that returns ErrFinalToken with a non-nil empty token when it sees \"x\". If Read returns \"x\" with nil and would report an I/O error on its next call, Scan returns true once with the empty token, subsequent calls return false without another Read, and Err is nil. If Read returns \"x\" and the I/O error together, Scan still returns true once with the empty token and subsequent calls return false, but Err reports the I/O error. In either ordering, a nil final token makes the stopping call return false immediately, and the sentinel branch bypasses advance validation.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 152,
        "fragment": "if err == ErrFinalToken {"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Should the promise that ErrFinalToken stops scanning with no error be limited to cases where no non-EOF error is already recorded?"
    },
    {
      "id": "zero-advance-token-guard",
      "text": "After a reader supplies one byte with no error, a splitter that always returns (0, a nonempty token, nil) lets repeated Scan calls return true without consuming the byte or reading again. If the reader supplies that byte together with io.EOF, the same splitter instead causes a panic on the maxConsecutiveEmptyReads+1st consecutive token return attempt. The guard counts zero-advance non-nil tokens after a recorded error regardless of token length.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 168,
        "fragment": "if s.err == nil || advance > 0 {"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Should the documented panic explicitly describe the recorded-error condition and its application to nonempty tokens?"
    },
    {
      "id": "buffer-boundary-eof-order",
      "text": "Before the first Scan, install a buffer of capacity 4 with max=4 and keep the default ScanLines splitter. If Read fills it with \"abcd\" and returns nil, Scan returns false with ErrTooLong before it can make a second Read that would report EOF. If Read returns those same four bytes together with io.EOF, Scan returns true with the four-byte token and then false with Err nil. After either first Scan, both Buffer and Split panic if called.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 200,
        "fragment": "if len(s.buf) >= s.maxTokenSize || len(s.buf) > maxInt/2 {"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Should the documented strict token-size limit describe the need for additional buffer space when EOF has yet to be reported?"
    },
    {
      "id": "lines-termination",
      "text": "With the default splitter and input \"a\\r\\n\\nlast\\r\" followed by EOF, successive successful scans produce \"a\", an empty line, and \"last\". The next scan returns false with Err nil. The final bare carriage return is stripped even though the documented end-of-line marker requires a newline. With input consisting only of \"a\\n\" followed by EOF, there is one successful scan and no extra empty token.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 368,
        "fragment": "return len(data), dropCR(data), nil"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Is stripping a bare final carriage return intended as a separate rule for unterminated lines?"
    },
    {
      "id": "token-storage-reuse",
      "text": "Use ScanLines with a two-byte buffer installed before scanning and a reader that gives \"a\\n\", then \"b\\n\", then EOF in separate reads. Save Bytes and Text after the first successful scan. The second scan reuses the same buffer positions, so the saved byte slice now contains \"b\" and aliases the current token, while the saved string still contains \"a\". The third scan returns false with Err nil and clears the current token, while the saved byte slice still contains \"b\".",
